<BaseLayout title="Editorial standards and change log" description="How AI Amigos sources, tests, reviews, corrects, and discloses practical AI resources."><div class="shell reading"><p class="eyebrow">Trust and editorial method</p><h1>Useful AI guidance should show its work.</h1><p class="lede">AI Amigos separates historical provenance, current testing, named review, and production proof so a visitor can see exactly what has—and has not—been verified.</p>
<h2>People and review ownership</h2><p><strong>AI Amigos Editorial Desk</strong> currently owns protocol structure, privacy boundaries, source checks, and correction handling. It is an organizational byline, not a claim of independent domain expertise. No individual reviewer or qualification is currently published; therefore legacy guides and high-stakes material remain outside the reviewed library. Individual names, qualifications, review scope, and conflicts must appear before their approval can make a guide or outcome public.</p>
<h2>Publication gate</h2><ol><li>Define one distinct job to be done and intended audience.</li><li>Use at least two authoritative external sources; prefer primary evidence.</li><li>Provide actionable steps, a worked example, limitations, and a practical artifact.</li><li>Name the author, reviewer, review date, next review date, and substantive changes.</li><li>Require explicit human approval before editorial publication.</li></ol>
<h2>Outcome and benchmark gate</h2><ol><li>A private project records the baseline, experiment versions, test cases, human decisions, and follow-up outcome.</li><li>Only deliberately sanitized fields can enter a proof-pack submission.</li><li>A reviewer checks privacy, claim scope, evidence lineage, and limitations.</li><li>Approved playbooks retain versions and correction history.</li><li>Benchmark cohorts remain suppressed below 10 reviewed comparable reports.</li></ol>
<h2>Review intervals</h2><ul><li>90 days: vendors, products, careers, and versioned technical guides.</li><li>180 days: evergreen foundations.</li><li>Gated review: healthcare, finance, safety, credentials, or other high-stakes claims.</li></ul>
<h2>Status language</h2><dl><dt><strong>Source-preserved</strong></dt><dd>The historical route and publication date remain, but the page has not passed the new evidence gate. It is noindex and excluded from discovery.</dd><dt><strong>Reviewed protocol</strong></dt><dd>The method, privacy boundary, sources, limitations, reviewer scope, and dates passed editorial review. It is not a claim of real-world outcome.</dd><dt><strong>Reviewed outcome</strong></dt><dd>A sanitized run report and follow-up measurement passed human review.</dd><dt><strong>Benchmark</strong></dt><dd>At least 10 comparable reviewed outcomes passed privacy, methodology, and editorial gates.</dd></dl>
<h2>Corrections and disclosure</h2><p>Send corrections, broken-tool reports, or source suggestions through <a href="/contact/">contact actions</a>. Material corrections are dated in the page change log. AI may help prepare drafts and checks; a person must approve publication. Funding, affiliate, vendor, or product relationships must be disclosed on the affected page.</p>
<h2>Public change log</h2><ul><li><strong>2026-08-24:</strong> Replaced the article-led shell with the verified practice network, compact brand and navigation, local project dashboard, four reviewed editorial protocols, benchmark suppression rules, monthly field challenges, legacy-guide quarantine, and extended RLS schema.</li><li><strong>2026-08-23:</strong> Added the local-first AI Practice OS workspace, seven-stage proof loop, verified playbook submission boundary, and optional account/BYOK architecture.</li><li><strong>2026-08-22:</strong> Added four tracks, five local-only tools, twenty templates, explicit legacy-guide status, and inactive-by-default feed and newsletter integrations.</li></ul></div></BaseLayout>
